Build screens from explicit state. Keep business rules independent of the UI. Isolate persistence behind an actor, and give asynchronous work a clear owner.

This guide defines the platform's iOS architecture and demonstrates it with a user profile: **show cached content, refresh remotely, persist a valid response, and publish the saved result**.

<Aside type="tip" title="Platform standard">
Use feature-oriented Clean Architecture with unidirectional data flow. SwiftUI renders state and emits actions. A main-actor ViewModel coordinates use cases. Repository contracts belong to the domain; data implementations own transport and persistence. Only immutable, Sendable values cross actor boundaries.
</Aside>

## Before you use the example

| Requirement | Reference configuration |
| :--- | :--- |
| Deployment target | iOS 17 or later for SwiftData and SwiftUI Observation integration |
| Language mode | Swift 6 with complete concurrency checking |
| Default actor isolation | Nonisolated for the feature target; UI types explicitly use `@MainActor` |
| Persistence | Local SwiftData store; CloudKit synchronization is outside this example |
| Dependencies | Foundation, Observation, SwiftUI, and SwiftData |
| Access control | Internal types in one target for a readable example; expose deliberate APIs when splitting packages |

Use your project's approved compiler and SDK versions. Compiler version, language mode, and default actor isolation are separate settings. If the host application uses main-actor default isolation, keep domain/data code in an appropriately configured target or adapt the declarations with the project's supported isolation syntax. Do not remove concurrency checking to silence errors.

**Scope:** The code includes the feature's domain, reference transport, persistence adapter, repository, presentation, and factories. The host app supplies navigation, environment configuration, authenticated networking, session coordination, migrations, and design tokens. These are explicit integration points, not implied SDK APIs.

**Reading path:** Review the boundaries and diagrams, follow the implementation tabs in order, then use the integration and test checklists.

## Architecture boundaries

| Layer | Owns | Boundary rule |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| Presentation | SwiftUI views, screen state, main-actor ViewModel | Calls use cases; never fetches SwiftData models or builds HTTP requests |
| Domain | Immutable business values, validation, repository protocol | No SwiftUI, UIKit, SwiftData, or transport implementations |
| Data | DTOs, HTTP adapter, repository implementation, model actor | Returns domain snapshots; persistence models stay inside the store actor |
| Composition root | Infrastructure lifetime, factories, configuration | Constructs dependencies and handles startup failures |

Directories organize code. Separate Swift Package targets and target dependencies enforce build-time isolation. Swift Package Manager manages packages and dependencies; it is not a dependency injection framework.

```text
Features/User/
├── Domain.swift          # Domain value, errors, repository boundary, use case
├── Remote.swift          # DTO, remote contract, reference URLSession adapter
├── Storage.swift         # SwiftData model, local contract, model actor
├── Repository.swift      # Account-scoped coordination and persistence-first result
├── Presentation.swift    # Screen state and @MainActor @Observable ViewModel
├── Views.swift           # Lifecycle-owning route and state-rendering content view
└── Composition.swift     # Factories and shared store construction
Tests/
└── ReferenceChecks.swift # Executable behavior checks; separate from app target
```

Keep files focused on one concern. Split the grouped declarations into smaller files as the feature grows; naming and dependencies matter more than a blanket one-type-per-file rule.

