### Composition.swift

Construct shared infrastructure once. Create screen-specific ViewModels through an injected factory. Initialization is throwing so the app can present a controlled recovery state instead of crashing.

```swift
import Foundation
import SwiftData

@MainActor
struct UserFeatureFactory {
    let repository: any UserRepository

    init(
        accountID: String,
        remote: any UserRemoteSource,
        local: any UserLocalStore
    ) throws {
        repository = try UserRepositoryImpl(
            accountID: accountID, remote: remote, local: local
        )
    }

    func makeViewModel() -> UserProfileViewModel {
        UserProfileViewModel(useCase: GetUserProfileUseCase(repository: repository))
    }
}

/// Call once from the app composition root; present a recovery UI if it throws.
/// Retain the store and share it across this feature's repositories.
@MainActor
func makeUserLocalStore(inMemory: Bool = false) throws -> SwiftDataUserLocalStore {
    let schema = Schema([UserPersistentModel.self])
    let configuration = ModelConfiguration(
        schema: schema, isStoredInMemoryOnly: inMemory
    )
    let container = try ModelContainer(for: schema, configurations: [configuration])
    return SwiftDataUserLocalStore(modelContainer: container)
}
```

<Aside type="tip" title="Hint · inject ownership, avoid a global singleton">
The app owns the model container, store, authenticated remote adapter, and account-scoped factory. Each screen gets its own ViewModel. This keeps previews and tests independent and makes account teardown explicit. Swift Package Manager can organize these types into targets, but does not create or inject their instances.
</Aside>

This example deliberately does not hardcode an API environment or create an unauthenticated `.shared` session in a feature factory. Inject the approved remote adapter from application configuration.

